Original commit body from 43056c8f14b58a7c83371230fa71b92c56dfe917:

The instrument rendered a disagreement's CARRIED value with a top-level
lookup on the serialised node. Nine of the ten attributes the merge pass
tracks are top-level in `Symbol.to_dict()`; `is_exported` is not — it
nests under `supply_chain`. So `node.get("is_exported")` was None for
every node and `docs/audits/0019` reported `null <- false` x41 for 41
records that carry `true`. A wrong instrument returns a plausible number
and exits 0 (LIVE.md rules 4 and 5); this one also produced a false
tracker filing against the merge pass, which was correct all along.

`_carried(node, attribute)` now returns (found, value): the top-level key
when there is one, else the attribute inside one of the record's field
groups, skipping the open payloads (`meta`, `quality`, `attribution`,
`alternatives`) whose keys are not field names. An attribute carried
nowhere renders `(not serialised)`, never `null`.

Only the shape column moves. The agree / disagree / one-sided counts come
from the ADR-0057 §6 slot and were always right, so no pinned tally in
either recorded fixture changes.

Evidence: the synthetic test goes red->green on `("null","false",1)` ->
`("true","false",1)`; the recorded aardvark-dns fixture now measures
`("true","false",41)` and its pin is updated; regenerating
docs/audits/0019 diffs exactly one cell. Three mutants killed (top-level
lookup restored; a miss returned as a value; the open payloads answering
the lookup).

Also corrected, since the wrong reading was committed and cited: 0019's
hand-written Reading bullet, ADR-0057 §5's landing note and WI-dajif's
tracker-item line, and both recorded fixtures' comments. And the core
package now covers `backend_agreement.py` 100% on its own — the
cross-language edge branch was covered only by the rust-analyzer
package's tests.

Closes WI-pofih

